  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The pattern remains favorable for daily\nshowers/storms for the entire upcoming week.\n\nA high pressure wedge across the area will begin to erode today,\nallowing a warm front to lift north. As this occurs, the\npersistent stratiform rain will end, and more scattered convective\nshowers/storms will occur. Temperatures will be warmer today \nthan the past couple of days thanks to this, with highs expected\nto reach into the 60s/70s. The timing of the warm front lifting\nnorth of the area could drastically affect high temperatures \ntoday if the passage occurs later in the day. Skies will remain \novercast, with showers/storms developing this afternoon across \nthe area as a shortwave trough moves through, increasing lift.\nNo severe weather is expected, and coverage of showers/storms\nwill be lower than the past couple of days.\n\nSoutherly moisture will continue to feed into the area on Monday,\nwith daily chances of showers/storms through midweek as a cold \nfront approaches from the west. This front stalls and becomes \neast/west oriented across the Mid-Atlantic Tuesday, which will \nkeep unsettled weather in place through late Wednesday. The \nstalled front drifts south for the late week, which will reduce \nbut not eliminate rain chances, especially for our southern \nlocations. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Isolated flash flooding will be possible due to\nrepeated rounds of showers/storms each day. \n\nWith plentiful moisture for the early part of the week, and a stalled\nfront across the area for the week ahead, repeated rounds of \nshowers/storms will continue. Very high precipitable water \nvalues near 2\" will allow for efficient rainfall to occur, which\nwill allow rain totals to add up, with 1-2\" of additional \nrainfall on top of what has already fallen the past couple of \ndays. Most of this rain will be absorbed by the ground and \nvegetation due to our area being in severe to extreme drought. \nHowever, urban, low-lying and flood-prone areas could be \nvulnerable to flash flooding. \n\nDue to this, WPC has placed our entire area in a Marginal Risk \nfor excessive rainfall for Monday through Wednesday night. The \nwestern mountains are in a Marginal Risk for today. Anywhere \nthat storms occur multiple days in a row will be most \nsusceptible to this risk, along with any training of storms. \nWhile the risk is low, rivers are beginning to rise with the \nrecent rainfall.",
